WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.421
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.721 --> 00:00:18.223
Unit thirty-three: Trust and Institutions. In this unit, you will discuss why people trust or distrust public institutions.

4
00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:20.821
institutional trust.

5
00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:27.689
institutional trust means confidence in organisations such as courts, banks and schools.

6
00:00:27.989 --> 00:00:31.254
Institutional trust has fallen in many countries.

7
00:00:31.954 --> 00:00:35.605
Your turn. Say: institutional trust.

8
00:00:38.305 --> 00:00:39.817
institutional trust.

9
00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:41.957
legitimacy.

10
00:00:42.657 --> 00:00:47.164
legitimacy means the quality of being accepted as fair and lawful.

11
00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:50.137
Elections give a government its legitimacy.

12
00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:54.209
Your turn. Say: legitimacy.

13
00:00:56.909 --> 00:00:58.120
legitimacy.

14
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.489
social contract.

15
00:01:01.189 --> 00:01:06.161
social contract means the unwritten agreement between citizens and the state.

16
00:01:06.461 --> 00:01:09.378
Paying taxes is part of the social contract.

17
00:01:10.078 --> 00:01:13.241
Your turn. Say: social contract.

18
00:01:15.941 --> 00:01:17.255
social contract.

19
00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:19.367
due process.

20
00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:24.621
due process means fair treatment through the normal legal system.

21
00:01:24.921 --> 00:01:28.592
Everyone deserves due process, including suspects.

22
00:01:29.292 --> 00:01:32.049
Your turn. Say: due process.

23
00:01:34.749 --> 00:01:35.855
due process.

24
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:38.350
public confidence.

25
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:43.093
public confidence means the trust that people have in an institution.

26
00:01:43.393 --> 00:01:46.264
The scandal damaged public confidence in the bank.

27
00:01:46.964 --> 00:01:50.754
Your turn. Say: public confidence.

28
00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:55.000
public confidence.

29
00:01:56.017 --> 00:01:57.529
transparency measure.

30
00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:02.852
transparency measure means an action that makes decisions open and visible.

31
00:02:03.152 --> 00:02:07.090
Publishing budgets online is a transparency measure.

32
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.965
Your turn. Say: transparency measure.

33
00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:15.026
transparency measure.

34
00:02:16.017 --> 00:02:17.633
integrity agency.

35
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:23.026
integrity agency means an independent body that investigates misconduct.

36
00:02:23.326 --> 00:02:26.463
The integrity agency investigated the contract.

37
00:02:27.163 --> 00:02:30.559
Your turn. Say: integrity agency.

38
00:02:33.259 --> 00:02:34.817
integrity agency.

39
00:02:35.817 --> 00:02:37.050
bureaucracy.

40
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:42.559
bureaucracy means a system of government with many complicated rules and officials.

41
00:02:42.859 --> 00:02:46.020
Too much bureaucracy slows down small businesses.

42
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.523
Your turn. Say: bureaucracy.

43
00:02:52.223 --> 00:02:53.352
bureaucracy.

44
00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.500
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.238
Rithy, why do you think institutional trust is falling in so many places?

46
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:07.037
Scandals, mostly. And slow bureaucracy frustrates ordinary people.

47
00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:11.673
Trust, once lost, is very hard to rebuild.

48
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:15.203
What can institutions actually do?

49
00:03:16.517 --> 00:03:22.269
Transparency measures help. Publishing budgets and decisions online, for example.

50
00:03:23.583 --> 00:03:26.059
And independent integrity agencies?

51
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:32.963
Crucial. Without due process and accountability, legitimacy disappears.

52
00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.421
So the social contract depends on fairness, not just power.

53
00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:43.051
Grammar focus: Fronting in formal argument.

54
00:03:43.351 --> 00:03:46.849
Fronting places the key idea at the beginning for impact.

55
00:03:47.149 --> 00:03:53.226
Trust, once lost, is hard to rebuild. Crucial to this is independent oversight.

56
00:03:53.526 --> 00:03:56.721
Without due process, legitimacy disappears.

57
00:03:57.021 --> 00:04:02.039
Fronting places your key idea first, giving formal arguments rhetorical force.

58
00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.376
Now read and listen. The Currency of Trust.

59
00:04:06.676 --> 00:04:12.065
Trust is often described as the invisible currency of a healthy society.

60
00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:19.297
When citizens trust institutions, they pay taxes, follow laws and cooperate more willingly.

61
00:04:20.583 --> 00:04:25.671
Surveys show, however, that institutional trust has declined in many countries.

62
00:04:26.983 --> 00:04:33.023
Corruption scandals, slow bureaucracy and unequal treatment are frequently cited as causes.

63
00:04:34.317 --> 00:04:40.159
Particularly damaging is the perception that rules apply differently to the powerful.

64
00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:47.664
Transparency measures, such as open data and public hearings, can help restore public confidence.

65
00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:53.411
Independent integrity agencies and due process are equally essential.

66
00:04:54.717 --> 00:05:00.745
Ultimately, legitimacy is earned through consistent fairness, not demanded through authority.

67
00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.726
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:05.026 --> 00:05:08.859
The quality of being accepted as fair and lawful is blank.

69
00:05:09.159 --> 00:05:13.777
A, legitimacy. B, bureaucracy. C, due process.

70
00:05:17.783 --> 00:05:20.526
The answer is A: legitimacy.

71
00:05:22.017 --> 00:05:23.935
Which sentence uses fronting?

72
00:05:24.235 --> 00:05:32.358
A, Independent oversight is crucial.. B, Crucial is independent oversight.. C, Oversight is independent..

73
00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.963
The answer is B: Crucial is independent oversight..

74
00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:44.599
Respect, once blank, is hard to regain.

75
00:05:44.899 --> 00:05:48.459
A, lose. B, lost. C, losing.

76
00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.264
The answer is B: lost.

77
00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.375
A system with many complicated rules and officials is blank.

78
00:05:59.675 --> 00:06:05.337
A, bureaucracy. B, integrity agency. C, social contract.

79
00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:12.023
The answer is A: bureaucracy.

80
00:06:13.517 --> 00:06:17.594
According to the text, what is particularly damaging to trust?

81
00:06:17.894 --> 00:06:25.135
A, open data. B, the perception that rules apply differently to the powerful. C, public hearings.

82
00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:33.924
The answer is B: the perception that rules apply differently to the powerful.

83
00:06:35.417 --> 00:06:40.379
Now it's your turn. Part one: What makes people trust an institution?

84
00:06:45.679 --> 00:06:51.487
Part two: Use fronting at least once, give two factors and one example.

85
00:06:57.787 --> 00:07:11.251
Most important is consistency: rules must apply to everyone. Transparency, too, plays a key role. When my university published its scholarship criteria online, students' confidence in the process grew immediately.

86
00:07:11.551 --> 00:07:13.864
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:19.557
Great work today! You learned eight new words and fronting in formal argument.

88
00:07:19.857 --> 00:07:23.334
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:23.634 --> 00:07:26.518
See you in Unit thirty-four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:26.818 --> 00:07:29.587
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

